    <h1>Enterprise Design Systems Engineering &amp; Governance</h1>
    <p class="project-subtitle" style="text-align: center; color: var(--color-muted); margin-bottom: 2rem;">Multi-Platform Token Architecture, Component Modularity &amp; Cross-Team Parity</p>

    <!-- Executive Scorecard -->
    <div class="project-meta-list">
      <p><span class="meta-label">Leadership Role:</span> Design Systems Lead &amp; System Architect</p>
      <p><span class="meta-label">Core Team:</span> Co-architected with Jason Chang, Emery Grearson, James Cradit &amp; Teja Telikicherla</p>
      <p><span class="meta-label">Platform Scope:</span> Enterprise Point of Sale (Associate Terminals &amp; Customer Screens), Responsive Web, and Mobile App</p>
      <p><span class="meta-label">Measured Impact:</span> Cut production design hand-off time by <strong>50%</strong>; eliminated visual drift between colleague and consumer touchpoints; established pre-approved, code-backed component pipelines across squads.</p>
    </div>

    <p class="project-intro">A design system isn't just a collection of Figma components—it's a living product and a contract between design and engineering. Drawing on my engineering background, I specialize in architecting design tokens, modular component systems, and clear hand-off governance. My priority is reducing design debt, breaking down technical silos, and speeding up delivery across enterprise software squads.</p>

    <h2>Initiative 1: The Multi-Platform POS System</h2>
    <p><em>Done in close collaboration with Jason Chang, Emery Grearson, James Cradit &amp; Teja Telikicherla.</em></p>

    <h3>The Challenge</h3>
    <p>Our Point of Sale infrastructure served two fundamentally different user groups: store colleagues operating complex enterprise management screens, and everyday retail shoppers interacting with customer-facing touchscreens. Over time, each platform had developed its own ad-hoc components, leading to visual inconsistencies, duplicated engineering effort, and maintenance nightmares.</p>

    <h3>The Architecture</h3>
    <p>We co-architected a unified design system that supported both environments from a shared token foundation:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Shared Tokens, Specialized Patterns:</strong> Colors, typography scales, touch-target minimums, and spacing values were tokenized across both platforms, while allowing specific interaction schemas (high-density tables for associates vs. large high-contrast buttons for shoppers).</li>
      <li><strong>Reduced Redundant Code:</strong> By sharing underlying components, engineering could build and maintain core logic once rather than writing separate UI libraries for each hardware screen.</li>
    </ul>

