    <h3>The Problem with Rigid Enterprise Components</h3>
    <p>Legacy components at CVS were often hardcoded for single use cases. When a designer needed to adapt a card or modal for a slightly different workflow, they were forced to detach the component or draw a custom one from scratch. Over a few quarters, this led to massive component sprawl and inconsistent production builds.</p>

    <h3>The Slot &amp; Variable Model</h3>
    <p>I led the rebuild of our atomic components by taking full advantage of modern Figma capabilities, specifically <strong>Variables, Modes, and Slot components</strong>:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Slot Components for Flexibility:</strong> By defining protected component shells with flexible "slots," designers could customize interior content for their specific needs without breaking the underlying padding, border radii, or token rules.</li>
      <li><strong>1-Click Breakpoint Transformations:</strong> Working with our front-end engineers, we established standardized responsive breakpoints. By tying these to Figma modes, I built components that designers could switch between mobile, tablet, and desktop views with a single click.</li>
    </ul>

    <p><strong>The Real-World Result:</strong> This single workflow enhancement cut the "production support" phase of design sprints in half. Designers could spend their time testing flows with real users instead of manually redrawing the same layout across three artboards.</p>

