    <h2>Initiative 4: The Loyalty Design System</h2>
    <p>As design lead for the Loyalty and Consumer Growth stream, I inherited legacy features built on disconnected components that made rapid A/B testing almost impossible. Every time our team wanted to experiment with a new reward prompt, it required weeks of custom design and development work.</p>

    <p>I directed the creation of an enterprise Loyalty Design System, unifying every atomic element across enrollment and reward redemption. This library gave our growth pods pre-approved building blocks that allowed them to ship and iterate experiments in days rather than sprints.</p>

    <h2>Leadership Retrospective</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Adoption is the Only True Metric:</strong> A design system with 500 perfect components is useless if product teams refuse to use it. Success comes from listening to product designers' real pain points and giving them components that actually save them time.</li>
      <li><strong>Engineering Must Co-Own the System:</strong> If developers view the design system as something "handed down from design," they won't defend it in code reviews. Involving engineers early in token naming and breakpoint definitions is non-negotiable.</li>
      <li><strong>Modularity Beats Rigidity:</strong> Hardcoded components always break down when edge cases arise. Building slot-based architectures provides the guardrails needed for brand consistency without crippling a designer's ability to solve unique user problems.</li>
    </ol>
